Q1) Deadlock Debugging 
    Exact condition causing deadlock
    P1 locks R1 and waits for R2.
    P2 locks R2 and waits for R1.
    Each process is waiting for the resource held by the other.
    This creates a Circular Wait, resulting in deadlock.
    Optimization
    Use a fixed resource ordering.
    Both processes should lock resources in the same order (e.g., R1 → R2).
   This removes the circular wait condition and prevents deadlock.
Q2) CPU Utilization Problem (10 Marks)
 (a) Cause of low CPU utilization

    The scheduler repeatedly shows:

    Ready → Waiting → Ready → Waiting → Ready

    This indicates that processes perform frequent I/O operations.

    CPU executes briefly.
    Process immediately requests I/O.
    CPU becomes idle while waiting.
    Process returns to ready state after I/O.
     Cycle repeats.

    This is an I/O-bound workload.

As a result:

   CPU utilization remains low (30%)
   CPU spends much time idle although processes exist.
  b) System-level optimization

   1. Increase Degree of Multiprogramming

    Load more processes so that while one waits for I/O, another uses the CPU.

Result:

    Better CPU utilization
    Reduced idle time

2. Improve I/O Performance

Examples:

   Asynchronous I/O
   DMA (Direct Memory Access)
   Faster SSDs
   I/O buffering and caching
   This reduces waiting time and increases CPU utilization
  
 Q3. Memory Bottleneck Debugging 
      Memory issue
      Frequent page faults slow down execution.
      Many threads access memory simultaneously.
      Poor memory locality causes repeated page loading.
      This leads to memory thrashing and performance degradation.
  Optimizations:
   Reduce heap usage by freeing unused memory and reusing objects.
   Use a thread pool or reduce stack size to decrease memory usage.
  
  Q4. Starvation Debugging 
  Root cause:
    P5 is in the lowest-priority queue (Q3).
    Higher-priority queues (Q1 and Q2) are always served first.
    Continuous arrival of high-priority processes prevents P5 from executing.
    This results in starvation.
Optimization:
     Apply Aging to gradually increase the priority of waiting processes.
     Reserve a small portion of CPU time for lower-priority queues.
     This ensures fairness and prevents starvation.
  
Q5. Context-Switch Overhead Debugging 
    Cause of high context switching
    Time quantum (1 ms) is smaller than the average CPU burst (2 ms).
    Threads are preempted before completing execution.
    Frequent I/O operations cause additional context switches.
    High context-switch overhead reduces system performance.
Optimizations:
    Increase the time quantum (e.g., 4–5 ms) to reduce preemption.
    Optimize I/O using asynchronous I/O, DMA, and buffering to reduce waiting and switching.
